{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct|tostring),(.fmax_mhz|tostring),(.cycles|tostring),(.lesson // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-4ddfa22\tBaseline retest for cores/bench\timprovement\t0.0\t127.03\t4491485\t\nhyp-20260710-001-r1s0\tFast-path ALU with selective M arithmetic\timprovement\t43.87\t182.75\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.87%): Separating the RV32I fast-path mux from gated M-extension multiply/divide logic, while deriving signed multiply high halves from one unsigned product, can substantially improve CoreMark by removing unconditional arithmetic fanout from ordinary integer and address-generation operations.\nhyp-20260710-001-r2s0\tShared RV32I arithmetic fast path\tregression\t0.0\t182.75\t4491485\t\nhyp-20260710-001-r3s0\tShared control-flow target adder\tregression\t-8.33\t167.53\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.33%): Sharing branch/JAL/JALR target arithmetic through a control-gated `target_base` mux and common `target_sum` can worsen CoreMark when the added EX selection and fanout cost more than the removed parallel adders, so arithmetic sharing must be evaluated against its control-path depth.\nhyp-20260710-001-r4s0\tDirect operand bypass muxing\tbroken\tnull\tnull\tnull\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Replacing encoded forwarding selects with direct EX/MEM/MEM/WB bypass hits must preserve freshest-writer priority and the forwarded values latched into RVFI source fields, or dependent instructions can expose stale registers and fail `reg_ch0`.\nhyp-20260710-001-r5s0\tExplicit load lane extraction\tregression\t-8.97\t166.36\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -8.97%): Replacing variable load shifts with explicit lane-case muxes did not pay off in CoreMark, showing that added MEM-stage selection and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings when the existing shift is already synthesized efficiently.\nhyp-20260710-001-r6s0\tSource-use-aware load-use interlock\tbroken\tnull\tnull\tnull\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `use_rs1`/`use_rs2` masks make load-use interlocks correctness-critical—any mismatch with the legal instruction decode or IF/ID timing can miss a required bubble and corrupt architectural register state, which `reg_ch0` catches.\nhyp-20260710-001-r7s0\tConsumer-specific EX operand mux factoring\tregression\t-9.55\t165.29\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.55%): Consumer-specific EX operand factoring can regress CoreMark when it duplicates the `fwd_rs1_sel`/`fwd_rs2_sel` mux trees for ALU inputs alongside canonical forwarded operands, so shortening nominal immediate/PC paths must be weighed against added mux replication and routing fanout.\nhyp-20260710-001-r8s0\tPredecoded class/sub-op ALU control\tregression\t-21.77\t142.96\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (regression, -21.77%): Predecoding ALU class/sub-operation fields did not pay off when the added ID/EX control fields and fanout outweighed removing EX range comparisons, showing that narrower control encoding helps only if it also reduces physical routing on the common CoreMark path.\nhyp-20260710-001-r9s0\tBit-factored M-extension ALU gating\tregression\t-55.91\t80.58\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (regression, -55.91%): Bit-factoring M-operation and multiply/divide predicates can regress even as it removes relational compares, showing that decode Boolean simplification is not a proxy for a shorter synthesized control-to-result path or lower fanout.\nhyp-20260710-001-r10s0\tShared branch compare flag cone\timprovement\t1.35\t185.22\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (improvement, +1.35%): A single 33-bit EX subtract with zero, borrow, and overflow-corrected signed-less-than flags can modestly improve CoreMark by sharing BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branch cones without adding pipeline latency.\nhyp-20260710-001-r11s0\tReuse ALU subtract flags for branches\tregression\t-17.2\t153.37\t4491485\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r11s0 (regression, -17.20%): Reusing ALU_SUB flags for branches regressed CoreMark, showing that crossing the ALU/EX interface with zero, borrow, and signed-less-than flag fanout can lengthen the branch-control path even when the subtract arithmetic is textually shared.\nhyp-20260710-001-r12s0\tZero-bubble load-use forwarding\tregression\t-40.24\t106.5\t4321614\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r12s0 (regression, -40.24%): Same-cycle MEM-to-EX load forwarding pulled dmem-ready/load-legality control and an extra bypass mux into common EX operand and redirect paths, so removing load-use bubbles did not repay the added timing cost in CoreMark.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
