hmetic, redirect suppression, and trap\\n  control behavior were left unchanged.\\n- No deviation from the hypothesis and no implementation concerns identified.\\n- Ordered Verilator lint passed with `core_pkg.sv` first, as required by the\\n  RTL source comments. The exact wildcard command reports pre-existing\\n  source-order typedef errors because it presents `core.sv` before\\n  `core_pkg.sv`.\\n- Local formal status: blocked before check generation. `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl\\n  CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` reached staging but the sandbox\\n  denied cleanup/writes under `formal/riscv-formal` with `Operation not\\n  permitted`; no formal counterexample was produced.\\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\" and .id\"'!=\"baseline-bench-4ddfa22\") | \"'\"\\\\n### \\\" + .id + \\\" | \\\" + .title + \\\" | outcome=\\\" + (.outcome//\\\"\\\") + \\\" delta=\\\" + ((.delta_pct//0)|tostring) + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nchanges: \\\" + ((.changes // []) | map(.file + \\\": \\\" + .description) | join(\\\" | \\\"))' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\" and .id\"'!=\"baseline-bench-4ddfa22\") | \"'\"\\\\n### \\\" + .id + \\\" | \\\" + .title + \\\" | outcome=\\\" + (.outcome//\\\"\\\") + \\\" delta=\\\" + ((.delta_pct//0)|tostring) + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nchanges: \\\" + ((.changes // []) | map(.file + \\\": \\\" + .description) | join(\\\" | \\\"))' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"\n### hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 | Fast-path ALU with selective M arithmetic | outcome=improvement delta=43.87\nRestructure the ALU into a narrow, timing-friendly RV32I fast path and a\nseparately selected M-extension path. Compute only the product signedness\nvariant required by the active M opcode, and isolate divide/remainder\narithmetic behind the M-op selection so non-M instructions do not traverse\nthe wide arithmetic network. Preserve the existing RV32M corner-case\nsemantics, including divide-by-zero and signed overflow, and keep the\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions identical. This should preserve IPC while\nshortening the common ALU critical path and reducing redundant logic.\n\nchanges: rtl/alu.sv: Split the ALU into an RV32I fast path and selectively evaluated M-extension arithmetic, preserving real and ALTOPS semantics.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 | Shared RV32I arithmetic fast path | outcome=regression delta=0.0\nRefactor the RV32I ALU fast path around one explicitly shared 32-bit\nadd/sub carry chain. Use its difference and carry/overflow information to\nimplement ADD, SUB, SLT, and SLTU, then select the bitwise, shift, and LUI\nresults through a factored operation-class mux. This should avoid duplicated\narithmetic cones and reduce control-to-result mux depth on the common path.\nKeep the existing gated M-extension datapath, divide corner cases, and\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions behaviorally identical.\n\nchanges: rtl/alu.sv: Implement a shared RV32I add/sub carry chain with compare derivation and a factored fast-path result mux, preserving all M-extension and ALTOPS semantics.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 | Shared control-flow target adder | outcome=regression delta=-8.33\nReplace the separate branch-target adder and JALR adder with one\ncontrol-gated target adder whose base is selected between PC and forwarded\nrs1.  Reuse its result for both branch and jump target formation, retaining\nJALR bit-zero clearing, misaligned-target trapping, redirect priority, and\nthe existing RVFI pc_next behavior.  This should reduce EX arithmetic area\nand control-to-target mux depth while leaving the retirement schedule and\nCoreMark IPC unchanged.\n\nchanges: rtl/ex_stage.sv: Fuse branch, JAL, and JALR target formation into one gated PC/rs1 plus immediate adder while preserving redirect and misalignment-trap semantics.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 | Direct operand bypass muxing | outcome=broken delta=0\nReplace the encoded forwarding selects with a direct, priority-ordered\noperand bypass network.  Generate EX/MEM and MEM/WB hit terms beside the\nregister-number comparisons, gate comparisons for operands unused by the\ndecoded instruction, and select the freshest 32-bit value with shallow\ncascaded muxes in EX.  Preserve EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority, x0 behavior,\nload-use stalling, branch/store operand values, and all RVFI-visible source\ndata.  Removing the select encode/decode layer should shorten the common\ndependency-to-ALU path and reduce control fanout without changing IPC.\n\nchanges: rtl/forward_unit.sv: Replace two-bit forwarding selects with source-use-gated EX/MEM and MEM/WB hit/data bypass signals, preserving freshest-writer priority and x0 suppression. | rtl/ex_stage.sv: Consume the direct priority bypass values for ALU, branch, and store operands, minimizing the operand mux depth while retaining exact forwarding behavior. | rtl/core.sv: Wire ID/EX operand values and decoded source-use controls into the direct bypass network without changing the pipeline or RVFI contract.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 | Explicit load lane extraction | outcome=regression delta=-8.97\nReplace the variable load shift with an explicit four-way byte-lane mux and\na two-way halfword-lane mux, and derive the byte masks from constant lookup\ncases. Keep word loads, sign/zero extension, misalignment traps, dmem\nhandshaking, and all RVFI memory fields unchanged. The narrower constant\nlane network should reduce LUT depth and routing on the load-to-writeback\npath without changing instruction timing or retirement behavior.\n\nchanges: rtl/mem_stage.sv: Implement explicit address-indexed load lane muxes and constant byte-mask cases, preserving exact RV32I load semantics, traps, handshakes, and RVFI fields.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 | Source-use-aware load-use interlock | outcome=broken delta=0\nDerive precise source-use bits for the IF/ID instruction from the existing\nlegal RV32IM decode and feed them into the hazard unit. Stall for load-use\nonly when the load rd is nonzero and matches a genuinely read rs1 or rs2;\npreserve the current one-cycle interlock for R-type, branch, store, and\naddress-generating consumers, along with all redirect, dmem-stall, x0, trap,\nand RVFI ordering behavior. This should eliminate false bubbles while\nleaving the optimized ALU and the common execute datapath unchanged.\n\nchanges: rtl/id_stage.sv: Expose decode-derived use_rs1/use_rs2 signals for the current IF/ID instruction, asserting them only for valid RV32IM consumers. | rtl/hazard_unit.sv: Gate load-use comparisons with the source-use signals while retaining exact load, x0, redirect, instruction-stall, and data-stall precedence. | rtl/core.sv: Wire the ID-stage source-use metadata into hazard detection without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 | Consumer-specific EX operand mux factoring | outcome=regression delta=-9.55\nFactor the EX operand network by consumer: select the immediate directly as\nthe ALU B operand when alu_src is set, and select PC directly as the ALU A\noperand for AUIPC, while retaining the existing freshest-writer forwarding\nmuxes for branch comparisons, stores, JALR, and RVFI source values. This\nshould shorten the dominant address-generation and integer ALU paths and\nreduce forwarding-control fanout without adding latency, changing hazards,\nor changing any architectural result or retirement metadata.\n\nchanges: rtl/ex_stage.sv: Refactor ALU operand selection so immediate and PC consumers bypass irrelevant forwarding mux levels while branches, stores, JALR, and RVFI retain exact forwarded operands.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 | Predecoded class/sub-op ALU control | outcome=regression delta=-21.77\nPredecode the ALU control in ID into an explicit M-extension class bit and\ncompact RV32I/M sub-operation fields, then pipeline those fields instead of\nmaking EX rediscover operation classes with range comparisons and a broad\n5-bit case select.  Implement the RV32I fast path with direct class/sub-op\nselects and keep the existing isolated multiply/divide path behind the M\nbit.  Preserve every RV32I and RV32M result, RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior,\nillegal-instruction handling, forwarding, and the unchanged single-cycle\nexecute/retirement schedule.  The expected gain is lower control-to-ALU\ndelay and reduced control fanout with no IPC cost.\n\nchanges: rtl/core_pkg.sv: Add compact predecoded ALU class/sub-operation fields to the pipeline control bundle while retaining all existing trap and memory controls. | rtl/decoder.sv: Generate the explicit M-class and compact ALU sub-operation controls only inside validated RV32IM opcode/funct matches, preserving strict illegal defaults. | rtl/id_stage.sv: Latch the predecoded ALU class/sub-operation controls into ID/EX without changing operand, hazard, or RVFI metadata timing. | rtl/alu.sv: Use the predecoded class/sub-operation selects to bypass 5-bit range/equality decoding on the common RV32I path while preserving real M arithmetic and ALTOPS semantics.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 | Bit-factored M-extension ALU gating | outcome=regression delta=-55.91\nReplace the ALU's range-based M-operation and divide-active predicates with\nbit-factored decode derived from the existing 5-bit operation encoding.\nKeep the externally visible encodings, decoder legality behavior, fast-path\nresults, multiply/divide corner cases, and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions\nunchanged.  This is a local combinational optimization: it should shorten\nthe control-to-result select and reduce selector fanout without adding\nlatency, changing forwarding, or altering IPC.\n\nchanges: rtl/alu.sv: Factor the existing ALU M-operation and divide gating predicates into Boolean decode from the current operation encoding, preserving real RV32M and ALTOPS behavior while reducing common-path range-comparator logic.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 | Shared branch compare flag cone | outcome=improvement delta=1.35\nReplace the EX branch comparison case with one 33-bit unsigned subtraction\nthat supplies zero, borrow, and signed-less-than flags. Select BEQ/BNE from\nthe zero flag and BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU from the corresponding signed or unsigned\nflag. Preserve the existing branch target adder, redirect priority,\nmisalignment trap handling, forwarding, and RVFI metadata. Sharing the\ncomparison flags should reduce duplicated relational/equality cones and\nfanout on the branch redirect path while leaving all non-branch execution\nbehavior unchanged.\n\nchanges: rtl/ex_stage.sv: Derive all RV32I branch predicates from a shared 33-bit rs1-rs2 subtract flag network while preserving exact signed/unsigned semantics and existing redirect/trap behavior.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r11s0 | Reuse ALU subtract flags for branches | outcome=regression delta=-17.2\nMake branch decode select the ALU subtract operation and expose the\nsubtract result flags (zero, unsigned borrow, and overflow-corrected signed\nless-than) from the ALU. Replace the separate EX branch-difference logic\nwith those flags, allowing the ALU's existing fast-path subtract carry\nchain to serve both its result and all branch predicates. Keep branch\ntarget arithmetic, forwarding, misalignment traps, RVFI metadata, and\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior unchanged. This preserves IPC and may reduce\nduplicated arithmetic/routing on the branch redirect path.\n\nchanges: rtl/alu.sv: Expose shared subtract zero, borrow, and signed-less-than flags alongside the existing RV32I fast-path result, preserving real and ALTOPS M-extension behavior. | rtl/decoder.sv: Set the validated conditional-branch control to use the ALU subtract operation without changing branch legality or operands. | rtl/ex_stage.sv: Consume ALU-provided compare flags for BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU and remove the duplicate EX branch subtract cone while retaining redirect and trap semantics.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r12s0 | Zero-bubble load-use forwarding | outcome=regression delta=-40.24\nAdd a precise MEM-to-EX load-result bypass that forwards the sign- or\nzero-extended load value directly from mem_stage into the EX operand mux.\nSuppress the load-use bubble only when the load response is ready and the\naccess is legal; retain the existing stall when dmemReady is low and retain\nnormal trap behavior for misaligned loads. Give this bypass priority over\nMEM/WB forwarding, carry the forwarded operands into the existing EX/MEM\nRVFI fields, and leave retirement order, memory masks, and all RV32M paths\nunchanged. This should improve CoreMark IPC without adding a new pipeline\nstage or speculative state.\n\nchanges: rtl/mem_stage.sv: Expose the already-computed load extraction result and a legal-load forward-valid signal without changing dmem handshakes, alignment traps, or RVFI capture. | rtl/forward_unit.sv: Add a highest-priority MEM load-data forwarding selection while retaining freshest-writer priority and x0 suppression. | rtl/ex_stage.sv: Consume the MEM load bypass in the EX operand muxes and latch the resulting operands for architectural execution and RVFI source data. | rtl/hazard_unit.sv: Gate only the load-use bubble on the legal, ready MEM bypass; continue freezing the pipeline for dmem stalls and trapping accesses. | rtl/core.sv: Wire mem_stage load-forward data and validity through forwarding and hazard control while preserving the single-channel RVFI contract.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r13s0 | Narrow post-EX control payload | outcome=regression delta=-7.55\nIntroduce a compact post-EX memory/writeback control record containing only\nmemory read/write, width/sign extension, register-write, load-select, and\nillegal/trap bits. Pack this reduced record into EX/MEM and MEM/WB while\nmapping the existing decoded controls into it at the EX boundary. Keep all\nEX branch, jump, ALU, and forwarding behavior unchanged, and preserve the\nexact memory masks, misalignment traps, RVFI fields, writeback selection,\nand single-cycle retirement semantics. Removing the unused control-flow\nfields from the broad pipeline bundles should reduce post-EX routing and\nimprove Fmax without adding bubbles or speculative state.\n\nchanges: rtl/core_pkg.sv: Add a compact post-EX memory/writeback control typedef and use it in EX/MEM and MEM/WB instead of carrying the full decode control record. | rtl/ex_stage.sv: Translate the validated EX control record into the reduced post-EX control fields while preserving jump results, misalignment traps, and all existing forwarding and retirement metadata. | rtl/mem_stage.sv: Consume and pipeline the reduced control record without changing dmem handshakes, byte masks, load extension, RVFI reporting, or writeback behavior.\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r14s0 | Share EX PC-plus-four result path | outcome=regression delta=-4.82\nFactor one explicitly shared EX pc_plus4 signal and use it for every\nreturn-address and sequential pc_next selection, while retaining separate\nbranch and JALR target arithmetic and the current redirect/trap priority.\nThe change should let synthesis place one dedicated PC incrementer and\nreduce duplicated adder outputs and mux fanout without adding a pipeline\nstage, changing forwarding, or carrying any new prediction metadata.\nMisaligned branch/jump traps must continue to report pc_plus4 and suppress\nthe redirect exactly as before.\n\nchanges: rtl/ex_stage.sv: Create one shared pc_plus4 expression and use it consistently for JAL/JALR link data, sequential pc_next, and misalignment-trap recovery while preserving existing target and redirect behavior.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
