{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r15s0.yaml && git diff --check -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r15s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-001-r15s0\ntitle: \"Conservative source-use load-use gating\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The load-use hazard currently compares the EX load destination against both\n  raw rs fields of every IF/ID instruction.  Immediate, load, JAL, LUI, and\n  AUIPC encodings reuse those bit positions for immediates, so dependent loads\n  can create unnecessary one-cycle bubbles.  CoreMark is sensitive to these\n  bubbles, while the pipeline and external contract can remain unchanged.\nhypothesis: |\n  Derive source-use masks directly from the IF/ID opcode and gate the existing\n  load-use comparisons: rs1 is used by OP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and\n  JALR; rs2 is used by OP, STORE, and BRANCH.  Keep the current exact\n  load-use, redirect, instruction-stall, data-stall, x0, and RVFI behavior.\n  For reserved or otherwise unrecognized encodings, conservatively mark both\n  fields as used so no symbolic instruction stream can bypass a required\n  interlock.  This should remove false CoreMark bubbles without adding a\n  pipeline stage or changing the common EX datapath.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add conservative IF/ID opcode source-use classification and gate load-use comparisons while preserving old behavior for unknown encodings and all stall/flush priorities.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Pass the existing IF/ID instruction bits into hazard detection without changing the top-level IO, pipeline timing contract, or RVFI retirement interface.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
