{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r5s0.yaml && git diff --check -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r5s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-001-r5s0\ntitle: \"Explicit load lane extraction\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The MEM stage currently extracts byte and halfword loads with a variable\n  32-bit right shift whose amount is formed from the effective address. That\n  datapath feeds writeback and the next-cycle forwarding path for dependent\n  loads, so a synthesized barrel-shifter-style cone may limit Fmax even\n  though only four byte lanes are legal.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the variable load shift with an explicit four-way byte-lane mux and\n  a two-way halfword-lane mux, and derive the byte masks from constant lookup\n  cases. Keep word loads, sign/zero extension, misalignment traps, dmem\n  handshaking, and all RVFI memory fields unchanged. The narrower constant\n  lane network should reduce LUT depth and routing on the load-to-writeback\n  path without changing instruction timing or retirement behavior.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Implement explicit address-indexed load lane muxes and constant byte-mask cases, preserving exact RV32I load semantics, traps, handshakes, and RVFI fields.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
