:51:33 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:07  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:07  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:07  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:08  Status: failed\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:08 (8)\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:06 (6)\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_42_56 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:42.6-42.59 step 20\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\\\nSBY 18:51:33 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\\\n\\\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r6s0/formal/last_run-90390.log\\\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r6s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-90390/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 6, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `use_rs1`/`use_rs2` masks make load-use interlocks correctness-critical\\\\u2014any mismatch with the legal instruction decode or IF/ID timing can miss a required bubble and corrupt architectural register state, which `reg_ch0` catches.\\\"}\\n18:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Constrained ALU completion skid during data stalls\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"A dmemReady stall currently freezes the entire five-stage machine even when\\\\nthe instruction behind the blocked load or store is an independent integer\\\\noperation. The earlier full elastic-memory queue was too broad and failed\\\\nliveness, while a store-only buffer added arbitration and ordering cost. A\\\\nmuch narrower completion structure can recover some of the lost overlap\\\\nwithout speculating or reordering any memory operation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"A dmemReady stall currently freezes the entire five-stage machine even when\\\\nthe instruction behind the blocked load or store is an independent integer\\\\noperation. The earlier full elastic-memory queue was too broad and failed\\\\nliveness, while a store-only buffer added arbitration and ordering cost. A\\\\nmuch narrower completion structure can recover some of the lost overlap\\\\nwithout speculating or reordering any memory operation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry in-order completion skid between EX and MEM that accepts only\\\\nyounger, non-memory, non-control ALU instructions while an older EX/MEM load\\\\nor store waits for io_dmemReady. Keep the blocked memory operation as the\\\\nretirement head, forbid younger loads, stores, branches, jumps, and illegal\\\\noperations from entering the skid, and stop the front end once the skid is\\\\nfull. After the memory completes, drain the skid entry into the normal\\\\nMEM/WB path before accepting another result. Add explicit forwarding from\\\\nthe skid entry and preserve age ordering, precise redirects, RVFI source\\\\nvalues, and register writeback. This limited bypass should hide short random\\\\ndmem stalls with modest area and control overhead while avoiding the timing\\\\nand correctness risks of a general completion queue.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Define a compact completion-skid payload and valid/age metadata for a younger ALU result while retaining the existing RVFI payload fields.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Integrate the one-entry ALU skid between EX and MEM, select the oldest completed result for writeback, and connect skid forwarding without changing the single-channel RVFI contract.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Expose a sideband candidate for non-memory, non-control ALU instructions and prevent skid capture of operations that could redirect, trap, or access memory.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add ordered drain/capture behavior so a blocked older data request remains the retirement head and the younger ALU completion reaches MEM/WB only after it.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace unconditional dmem-stall freezing with skid-full and dependency-aware backpressure, while keeping memory ordering, load-use hazards, redirect flushes, and forward progress precise.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Give the completion skid highest forwarding priority for dependent ALU operands, with x0 and valid gating and older EX/MEM/MEM/WB priority preserved when the skid is absent.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Define a compact completion-skid payload and valid/age metadata for a younger ALU result while retaining the existing RVFI payload fields.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Integrate the one-entry ALU skid between EX and MEM, select the oldest completed result for writeback, and connect skid forwarding without changing the single-channel RVFI contract.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Expose a sideband candidate for non-memory, non-control ALU instructions and prevent skid capture of operations that could redirect, trap, or access memory.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add ordered drain/capture behavior so a blocked older data request remains the retirement head and the younger ALU completion reaches MEM/WB only after it.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace unconditional dmem-stall freezing with skid-full and dependency-aware backpressure, while keeping memory ordering, load-use hazards, redirect flushes, and forward progress precise.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Give the completion skid highest forwarding priority for dependent ALU operands, with x0 and valid gating and older EX/MEM/MEM/WB priority preserved when the skid is absent.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 6, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry ALU completion skid must separate memory completion, skid capture/drain, and EX/MEM clearing while preserving the single MEM/WB retirement slot and age ordering, or `dmemReady` transitions can corrupt CoreMark execution.\\\"}\\n19:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tokenized exact-PC loop target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The five-stage core still pays an EX redirect and front-end flush for\\\\nrepeatedly taken CoreMark loops. Earlier BTFNT, RAS, and tagged BTB attempts\\\\nshowed that decoding in IF, carrying a 32-bit prediction through the\\\\npipeline, or adding speculative recovery state can erase the IPC benefit\\\\nand damage Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The five-stage core still pays an EX redirect and front-end flush for\\\\nrepeatedly taken CoreMark loops. Earlier BTFNT, RAS, and tagged BTB attempts\\\\nshowed that decoding in IF, carrying a 32-bit prediction through the\\\\npipeline, or adding speculative recovery state can erase the IPC benefit\\\\nand damage Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry exact-PC last-target predictor specialized for recurring\\\\ncontrol-transfer PCs. IF uses the registered tag/target hit to fetch the\\\\npredicted target, but carries only a one-bit prediction token with the\\\\ninstruction; EX re-looks up the target and compares it with the resolved\\\\nnext PC. A token is trusted only when the EX lookup still hits and agrees,\\\\nso table replacement, not-taken exits, misaligned targets, and aliases\\\\nalways take the normal precise redirect path. Train only on valid taken\\\\nbranches and jumps, keeping the target mux and pipeline sideband smaller\\\\nthan the prior metadata-heavy BTBs while removing steady-state loop flushes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a single prediction-token bit to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles without carrying a 32-bit predicted target through the pipeline.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement a reset-safe one-entry exact-PC target table with IF and EX lookup ports and taken-transfer training.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use the predictor hit target for the next fetch while preserving imem stalls, EX redirect priority, and an atomic per-instruction prediction token.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pipeline the prediction token alongside the instruction and decoded control state.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Recompute the predicted target from the EX lookup, suppress redirects only for tokenized agreeing predictions, and redirect precisely on misses or exits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the predictor lookup/training and token-aware redirect path through the existing single-issue pipeline without changing the RVFI contract.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a single prediction-token bit to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles without carrying a 32-bit predicted target through the pipeline.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement a reset-safe one-entry exact-PC target table with IF and EX lookup ports and taken-transfer training.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use the predictor hit target for the next fetch while preserving imem stalls, EX redirect priority, and an atomic per-instruction prediction token.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pipeline the prediction token alongside the instruction and decoded control state.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Recompute the predicted target from the EX lookup, suppress redirects only for tokenized agreeing predictions, and redirect precisely on misses or exits.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the predictor lookup/training and token-aware redirect path through the existing single-issue pipeline without changing the RVFI contract.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 401.44, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -1.34, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 41.94, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 173.55, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4323203, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 7671, \\\"ff\\\": 1928, \\\"seeds\\\": [173.55, 166.67, 182.48], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-10T21:58:45.127087Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 6, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -1.34%): Even a one-entry exact-PC target table with only a one-bit pipeline token can regress CoreMark when IF target selection plus EX re-lookup/agreement and miss-repair redirects cost more than the repeatedly taken loop flushes it removes.\\\"}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\\\\\"micro_opt\\\\\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct|tostring),(.fitness|tostring),(.fmax_mhz|tostring),(.cycles|tostring),(.lesson // \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\\\\\"micro_opt\\\\\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct|tostring),(.fitness|tostring),(.fmax_mhz|tostring),(.cycles|tostring),(.lesson // \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"baseline-bench-4ddfa22\\tBaseline retest for cores/bench\\timprovement\\t0.0\\t282.82\\t127.03\\t4491485\\t\\nhyp-20260710-001-r1s0\\tFast-path ALU with selective M arithmetic\\timprovement\\t43.87\\t406.88\\t182.75\\t4491485\\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.87%): Separating the RV32I fast-path mux from gated M-extension multiply/divide logic, while deriving signed multiply high halves from one unsigned product, can substantially improve CoreMark by removing unconditional arithmetic fanout from ordinary integer and address-generation operations.\\nhyp-20260710-001-r2s0\\tShared RV32I arithmetic fast path\\tregression\\t0.0\\t406.88\\t182.75\\t4491485\\t\\nhyp-20260710-001-r3s0\\tShared control-flow target adder\\tregression\\t-8.33\\t372.99\\t167.53\\t4491485\\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.33%): Sharing branch/JAL/JALR target arithmetic through a control-gated `target_base` mux and common `target_sum` can worsen CoreMark when the added EX selection and fanout cost more than the removed parallel adders, so arithmetic sharing must be evaluated against its control-path depth.\\nhyp-20260710-001-r4s0\\tDirect operand bypass muxing\\tbroken\\tnull\\tnull\\tnull\\tnull\\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Replacing encoded forwarding selects with direct EX/MEM/MEM/WB bypass hits must preserve freshest-writer priority and the forwarded values latched into RVFI source fields, or dependent instructions can expose stale registers and fail `reg_ch0`.\\nhyp-20260710-001-r5s0\\tExplicit load lane extraction\\tregression\\t-8.97\\t370.39\\t166.36\\t4491485\\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -8.97%): Replacing variable load shifts with explicit lane-case muxes did not pay off in CoreMark, showing that added MEM-stage selection and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings when the existing shift is already synthesized efficiently.\\nhyp-20260710-001-r6s0\\tSource-use-aware load-use interlock\\tbroken\\tnull\\tnull\\tnull\\tnull\\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `use_rs1`/`use_rs2` masks make load-use interlocks correctness-critical—any mismatch with the legal instruction decode or IF/ID timing can miss a required bubble and corrupt architectural register state, which `reg_ch0` catches.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The prior `micro_opt` record shows the only strong win came from shortening the common ALU path; the attempted load-use and bypass changes were correctness-sensitive, and lane extraction/control-flow adder sharing regressed. I’m checking the current datapath structure for a smaller timing-only optimization that avoids adding pipeline latency or new metadata.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n \\\\\\\"critical|delay|timing|fmax|ALU|forward|hazard|decode|mux|fanout\\\\\\\" cores/bench/experiments cores/bench/README.md cores/bench/ARCHITECTURE.md 2>/dev/null | head -n 240; find cores/bench -maxdepth 3 -type f \\\\\\\\( -name '*report*' -o -name '*.rpt' -o -name '*.log' \\\\\\\\) -print | head -n 80\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n \\\\\\\"critical|delay|timing|fmax|ALU|forward|hazard|decode|mux|fanout\\\\\\\" cores/bench/experiments cores/bench/README.md cores/bench/ARCHITECTURE.md 2>/dev/null | head -n 240; find cores/bench -maxdepth 3 -type f \\\\\\\\( -name '*report*' -o -name '*.rpt' -o -name '*.log' \\\\\\\\) -print | head -n 80\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:1:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-4ddfa22\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-10T19:51:27.296977Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Fast-path ALU with selective M arithmetic\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current combinational ALU instantiates and evaluates three 64-bit\\\\nproducts in parallel, while the M-extension divide/remainder operators are\\\\nalso part of the same wide result mux. CoreMark is dominated by ordinary\\\\ninteger, address-generation, compare, and control operations, so this\\\\nunconditional arithmetic fanout is a likely Fmax limiter and consumes LUTs\\\\nwithout improving its iteration rate.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current combinational ALU instantiates and evaluates three 64-bit\\\\nproducts in parallel, while the M-extension divide/remainder operators are\\\\nalso part of the same wide result mux. CoreMark is dominated by ordinary\\\\ninteger, address-generation, compare, and control operations, so this\\\\nunconditional arithmetic fanout is a likely Fmax limiter and consumes LUTs\\\\nwithout improving its iteration rate.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Restructure the ALU into a narrow, timing-friendly RV32I fast path and a\\\\nseparately selected M-extension path. Compute only the product signedness\\\\nvariant required by the active M opcode, and isolate divide/remainder\\\\narithmetic behind the M-op selection so non-M instructions do not traverse\\\\nthe wide arithmetic network. Preserve the existing RV32M corner-case\\\\nsemantics, including divide-by-zero and signed overflow, and keep the\\\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions identical. This should preserve IPC while\\\\nshortening the common ALU critical path and reducing redundant logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Split the ALU into an RV32I fast path and selectively evaluated M-extension arithmetic, preserving real and ALTOPS semantics.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Split the ALU into an RV32I fast path and selectively evaluated M-extension arithmetic, preserving real and ALTOPS semantics.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 406.88, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 43.87, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 43.87, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 182.75, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 7513, \\\"ff\\\": 1871, \\\"seeds\\\": [170.33, 182.75, 183.42], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-10T20:07:22.477014Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 1, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.87%): Separating the RV32I fast-path mux from gated M-extension multiply/divide logic, while deriving signed multiply high halves from one unsigned product, can substantially improve CoreMark by removing unconditional arithmetic fanout from ordinary integer and address-generation operations.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:3:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decoupled instruction prefetch FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current fetch stage stalls the entire five-stage pipeline whenever\\\\nio_imemReady is low, so the benchmark's random instruction-bus stalls\\\\ndirectly reduce retired instructions per cycle. CoreMark has enough\\\\nstraight-line execution between redirects for a small queue to hide many\\\\nof those bubbles without changing the external bus contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current fetch stage stalls the entire five-stage pipeline whenever\\\\nio_imemReady is low, so the benchmark's random instruction-bus stalls\\\\ndirectly reduce retired instructions per cycle. CoreMark has enough\\\\nstraight-line execution between redirects for a small queue to hide many\\\\nof those bubbles without changing the external bus contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a four-entry tagged instruction prefetch FIFO between imem and ID.\\\\nKeep a fetch address generator filling the FIFO whenever io_imemReady is\\\\nhigh, let ID consume queued instructions independently of transient fetch\\\\nbackpressure, and flush the queue plus restart its fetch pointer on an EX\\\\nredirect. Preserve the existing in-order valid/PC flow and make the queue\\\\nimpose backpressure only when empty, so branch recovery and RVFI retirement\\\\nremain architecturally identical while instruction stalls are overlapped.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single-PC fetch hold behavior with a four-entry tagged instruction prefetch FIFO, including ready-aware fill, dequeue, redirect flush, and precise PC/valid output.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Reconnect front-end stall and flush handling so downstream hazards only stop FIFO consumption when necessary, while imem backpressure does not freeze already-buffered instructions.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Separate instruction-queue-empty backpressure from imemReady stalls and retain redirect/load-use/dmem stall precedence without allowing duplicate or skipped fetches.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single-PC fetch hold behavior with a four-entry tagged instruction prefetch FIFO, including ready-aware fill, dequeue, redirect flush, and precise PC/valid output.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Reconnect front-end stall and flush handling so downstream hazards only stop FIFO consumption when necessary, while imem backpressure does not freeze already-buffered instructions.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Separate instruction-queue-empty backpressure from imemReady stalls and retain redirect/load-use/dmem stall precedence without allowing duplicate or skipped fetches.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 303.05, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 7.15, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 7.15, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 142.35, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4697199, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9969, \\\"ff\\\": 1966, \\\"seeds\\\": [134.83, 142.86, 142.35], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-10T20:11:33.613459Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 1, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 (regression, +7.15%): A small prefetch FIFO is not automatically beneficial when EX redirects flush every queued instruction and FIFO-empty refill inserts ID bubbles, so redirect frequency and refill cost can outweigh hiding random imem backpressure in CoreMark.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:4:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Fetch-time backward-taken branch prediction\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The five-stage bench core resolves every conditional branch in EX and\\\\notherwise fetches sequentially, so taken loop branches pay a redirect and\\\\nflush penalty. CoreMark contains many tight backward loops, while the FPGA\\\\nimplementation has little LUT headroom for a sizeable BTB or history table.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The five-stage bench core resolves every conditional branch in EX and\\\\notherwise fetches sequentially, so taken loop branches pay a redirect and\\\\nflush penalty. CoreMark contains many tight backward loops, while the FPGA\\\\nimplementation has little LUT headroom for a sizeable BTB or history table.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a low-area static BTFNT predictor in IF: recognize conditional branch\\\\nopcodes from the fetched instruction, predict negative-offset branches taken,\\\\nand form their immediate target during fetch. Carry prediction metadata with\\\\nthe instruction and make EX redirect only when the predicted next PC differs\\\\nfrom the resolved next PC, including the predicted-taken/actually-not-taken\\\\nloop-exit case. This should remove most taken-loop bubbles without adding\\\\npredictor state or weakening architectural recovery and RVFI behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted-next-PC and prediction-valid metadata.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement fetch-time BTFNT recognition and direct B-immediate target generation, while preserving stall and redirect priority.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pipeline the branch prediction metadata alongside the decoded instruction.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Compare predicted and resolved branch next PCs, redirect on misprediction, and retain misalignment/trap recovery semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the prediction-aware redirect and flush behavior through the existing pipeline control path.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted-next-PC and prediction-valid metadata.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement fetch-time BTFNT recognition and direct B-immediate target generation, while preserving stall and redirect priority.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pipeline the branch prediction metadata alongside the decoded instruction.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Compare predicted and resolved branch next PCs, redirect on misprediction, and retain misalignment/trap recovery semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the prediction-aware redirect and flush behavior through the existing pipeline control path.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 348.58, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 23.25, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 23.25, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 150.22, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4309463, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9985, \\\"ff\\\": 1900, \\\"seeds\\\": [150.4, 150.22, 141.42], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-10T20:03:36.714593Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 1, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.25%): A fetch-time BTFNT predictor can lose badly when `imem_data` branch decode, immediate formation, target addition, and prediction metadata extend the PC/IF critical path and recovery overhead enough to outweigh saved backward-loop redirects in CoreMark.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:5:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Shared RV32I arithmetic fast path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The routed critical path is in the execute ALU result, running from\\\\nID/EX control bits through the ordinary arithmetic selection and adder\\\\nchain. CoreMark is dominated by ADD/ADDI address and integer operations,\\\\nso reducing this common path should improve Fmax without changing IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The routed critical path is in the execute ALU result, running from\\\\nID/EX control bits through the ordinary arithmetic selection and adder\\\\nchain. CoreMark is dominated by ADD/ADDI address and integer operations,\\\\nso reducing this common path should improve Fmax without changing IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Refactor the RV32I ALU fast path around one explicitly shared 32-bit\\\\nadd/sub carry chain. Use its difference and carry/overflow information to\\\\nimplement ADD, SUB, SLT, and SLTU, then select the bitwise, shift, and LUI\\\\nresults through a factored operation-class mux. This should avoid duplicated\\\\narithmetic cones and reduce control-to-result mux depth on the common path.\\\\nKeep the existing gated M-extension datapath, divide corner cases, and\\\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions behaviorally identical.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement a shared RV32I add/sub carry chain with compare derivation and a factored fast-path result mux, preserving all M-extension and ALTOPS semantics.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Implement a shared RV32I add/sub carry chain with compare derivation and a factored fast-path result mux, preserving all M-extension and ALTOPS semantics.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 406.88, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 43.87, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 182.75, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 7513, \\\"ff\\\": 1871, \\\"seeds\\\": [170.33, 182.75, 183.42], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-10T20:19:27.336317Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 2}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:6:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Elastic in-order data-memory completion queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current MEM-stage handshake freezes PC, ID/EX, and EX/MEM whenever\\\\nio_dmemReady is low, so a transient data-bus stall blocks unrelated ALU\\\\ninstructions and directly lowers CoreMark instructions per cycle. The\\\\nrecent fetch FIFO result also shows that buffering only the front end is\\\\ninsufficient when the dominant stall is downstream.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current MEM-stage handshake freezes PC, ID/EX, and EX/MEM whenever\\\\nio_dmemReady is low, so a transient data-bus stall blocks unrelated ALU\\\\ninstructions and directly lowers CoreMark instructions per cycle. The\\\\nrecent fetch FIFO result also shows that buffering only the front end is\\\\ninsufficient when the dominant stall is downstream.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Restructure the memory boundary as a small elastic in-order queue with a\\\\ndedicated completion/retirement head. Allow younger independent ALU\\\\ninstructions to execute into queue entries while an older load or store\\\\nwaits for io_dmemReady, but prevent younger memory operations from passing\\\\nan older memory operation and hold dependent consumers until the load data\\\\nis available. Retire only the queue head, preserving RVFI order, precise\\\\ntraps, register writeback, and architectural memory ordering. This should\\\\noverlap random dmem backpressure with useful integer work without adding a\\\\nfetch-time critical path or changing the external bus contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add the metadata needed for elastic memory/completion entries, including age, completion, and deferred load/store state while preserving the existing RVFI payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single blocking MEM/WB boundary with a small in-order memory/completion queue that holds stalled data requests, accepts younger non-memory results, and exposes only the oldest completed entry to writeback and RVFI.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generate queue-full, older-memory-order, and load-result dependency stalls instead of freezing the complete pipeline on every dmemReady low cycle.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire elastic queue ready/valid flow through EX, MEM, WB, and retirement so dmem backpressure overlaps independent execution while redirects and RVFI remain precise.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add the metadata needed for elastic memory/completion entries, including age, completion, and deferred load/store state while preserving the existing RVFI payload.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single blocking MEM/WB boundary with a small in-order memory/completion queue that holds stalled data requests, accepts younger non-memory results, and exposes only the oldest completed entry to writeback and RVFI.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generate queue-full, older-memory-order, and load-result dependency stalls instead of freezing the complete pipeline on every dmemReady low cycle.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire elastic queue ready/valid flow through EX, MEM, WB, and retirement so dmem backpressure overlaps independent execution while redirects and RVFI remain precise.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"formal_failed: liveness_ch0\\\\nsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:33 (33)\\\\nSBY 17:35:35 [insn_xori_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:28 (28)\\\\nSBY 17:35:35 [insn_xori_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\\\nSBY 17:35:35 [insn_xori_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\\\nSBY 17:35:35 [insn_xori_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:37  Status: passed\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:38 (38)\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:33 (33)\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\\\nSBY 17:35:38 [insn_sw_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\\\n\\\\nFormal: 51 passed, 2 failed\\\\nFailed: liveness_ch0 reg_ch0\\\\n\\\\n--- liveness_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\\\nSBY 17:31:47 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 8..\\\\nSBY 17:31:47 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\\\nSBY 17:31:47 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\\\nSBY 17:31:47 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\\\nSBY 17:31:47 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 12..\\\\nSBY 17:31:47 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 13..\\\\nSBY 17:31:48 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 14..\\\\nSBY 17:31:48 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 15..\\\\nSBY 17:31:48 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 16..\\\\nSBY 17