{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | \\\"\\\\n### \\\\(.id) \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis)\\\\nCHANGES:\\\\n\\\\(.changes|map(.file+\\\" :: \\\"+.description)|join(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"jq: error (at cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:1): Cannot iterate over null (null)\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 Fast-path ALU with selective M arithmetic\nMOTIVATION:\nThe current combinational ALU instantiates and evaluates three 64-bit\nproducts in parallel, while the M-extension divide/remainder operators are\nalso part of the same wide result mux. CoreMark is dominated by ordinary\ninteger, address-generation, compare, and control operations, so this\nunconditional arithmetic fanout is a likely Fmax limiter and consumes LUTs\nwithout improving its iteration rate.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n{\"motivation\":\"The current combinational ALU instantiates and evaluates three 64-bit\\nproducts in parallel, while the M-extension divide/remainder operators are\\nalso part of the same wide result mux. CoreMark is dominated by ordinary\\ninteger, address-generation, compare, and control operations, so this\\nunconditional arithmetic fanout is a likely Fmax limiter and consumes LUTs\\nwithout improving its iteration rate.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Restructure the ALU into a narrow, timing-friendly RV32I fast path and a\\nseparately selected M-extension path. Compute only the product signedness\\nvariant required by the active M opcode, and isolate divide/remainder\\narithmetic behind the M-op selection so non-M instructions do not traverse\\nthe wide arithmetic network. Preserve the existing RV32M corner-case\\nsemantics, including divide-by-zero and signed overflow, and keep the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions identical. This should preserve IPC while\\nshortening the common ALU critical path and reducing redundant logic.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":4,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/alu.sv\",\"description\":\"Split the ALU into an RV32I fast path and selectively evaluated M-extension arithmetic, preserving real and ALTOPS semantics.\"}]}\nCHANGES:\nrtl/alu.sv :: Split the ALU into an RV32I fast path and selectively evaluated M-extension arithmetic, preserving real and ALTOPS semantics.\nNOTES:\n\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 Shared RV32I arithmetic fast path\nMOTIVATION:\nThe routed critical path is in the execute ALU result, running from\nID/EX control bits through the ordinary arithmetic selection and adder\nchain. CoreMark is dominated by ADD/ADDI address and integer operations,\nso reducing this common path should improve Fmax without changing IPC.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n{\"motivation\":\"The routed critical path is in the execute ALU result, running from\\nID/EX control bits through the ordinary arithmetic selection and adder\\nchain. CoreMark is dominated by ADD/ADDI address and integer operations,\\nso reducing this common path should improve Fmax without changing IPC.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Refactor the RV32I ALU fast path around one explicitly shared 32-bit\\nadd/sub carry chain. Use its difference and carry/overflow information to\\nimplement ADD, SUB, SLT, and SLTU, then select the bitwise, shift, and LUI\\nresults through a factored operation-class mux. This should avoid duplicated\\narithmetic cones and reduce control-to-result mux depth on the common path.\\nKeep the existing gated M-extension datapath, divide corner cases, and\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions behaviorally identical.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":4,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/alu.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement a shared RV32I add/sub carry chain with compare derivation and a factored fast-path result mux, preserving all M-extension and ALTOPS semantics.\"}]}\nCHANGES:\nrtl/alu.sv :: Implement a shared RV32I add/sub carry chain with compare derivation and a factored fast-path result mux, preserving all M-extension and ALTOPS semantics.\nNOTES:\n\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 Shared control-flow target adder\nMOTIVATION:\nThe successful ALU optimization showed that redundant arithmetic cones on\nthe common EX path materially limit Tang Nano 20K Fmax.  The current EX\nstage independently computes pc+imm for branches and JAL and rs1+imm for\nJALR, even though only one control-flow target is needed for an instruction.\nThose parallel adders and the following mux add fanout to the same stage\nwithout improving ordinary integer throughput.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n{\"motivation\":\"The successful ALU optimization showed that redundant arithmetic cones on\\nthe common EX path materially limit Tang Nano 20K Fmax.  The current EX\\nstage independently computes pc+imm for branches and JAL and rs1+imm for\\nJALR, even though only one control-flow target is needed for an instruction.\\nThose parallel adders and the following mux add fanout to the same stage\\nwithout improving ordinary integer throughput.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Replace the separate branch-target adder and JALR adder with one\\ncontrol-gated target adder whose base is selected between PC and forwarded\\nrs1.  Reuse its result for both branch and jump target formation, retaining\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, misaligned-target trapping, redirect priority, and\\nthe existing RVFI pc_next behavior.  This should reduce EX arithmetic area\\nand control-to-target mux depth while leaving the retirement schedule and\\nCoreMark IPC unchanged.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Fuse branch, JAL, and JALR target formation into one gated PC/rs1 plus immediate adder while preserving redirect and misalignment-trap semantics.\"}]}\nCHANGES:\nrtl/ex_stage.sv :: Fuse branch, JAL, and JALR target formation into one gated PC/rs1 plus immediate adder while preserving redirect and misalignment-trap semantics.\nNOTES:\n\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 Direct operand bypass muxing\nMOTIVATION:\nThe successful ALU optimization has removed the largest unconditional\narithmetic cone, leaving the EX operand path as a likely next limiter.\nForwarding currently performs register comparisons, encodes the result as\na two-bit select, and then decodes that select through a 32-bit mux for each\noperand.  CoreMark's dependent integer and address-generation instructions\nexercise this path frequently.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n{\"motivation\":\"The successful ALU optimization has removed the largest unconditional\\narithmetic cone, leaving the EX operand path as a likely next limiter.\\nForwarding currently performs register comparisons, encodes the result as\\na two-bit select, and then decodes that select through a 32-bit mux for each\\noperand.  CoreMark's dependent integer and address-generation instructions\\nexercise this path frequently.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Replace the encoded forwarding selects with a direct, priority-ordered\\noperand bypass network.  Generate EX/MEM and MEM/WB hit terms beside the\\nregister-number comparisons, gate comparisons for operands unused by the\\ndecoded instruction, and select the freshest 32-bit value with shallow\\ncascaded muxes in EX.  Preserve EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority, x0 behavior,\\nload-use stalling, branch/store operand values, and all RVFI-visible source\\ndata.  Removing the select encode/decode layer should shorten the common\\ndependency-to-ALU path and reduce control fanout without changing IPC.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Replace two-bit forwarding selects with source-use-gated EX/MEM and MEM/WB hit/data bypass signals, preserving freshest-writer priority and x0 suppression.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Consume the direct priority bypass values for ALU, branch, and store operands, minimizing the operand mux depth while retaining exact forwarding behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire ID/EX operand values and decoded source-use controls into the direct bypass network without changing the pipeline or RVFI contract.\"}]}\nCHANGES:\nrtl/forward_unit.sv :: Replace two-bit forwarding selects with source-use-gated EX/MEM and MEM/WB hit/data bypass signals, preserving freshest-writer priority and x0 suppression.\nrtl/ex_stage.sv :: Consume the direct priority bypass values for ALU, branch, and store operands, minimizing the operand mux depth while retaining exact forwarding behavior.\nrtl/core.sv :: Wire ID/EX operand values and decoded source-use controls into the direct bypass network without changing the pipeline or RVFI contract.\nNOTES:\n\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 Explicit load lane extraction\nMOTIVATION:\nThe MEM stage currently extracts byte and halfword loads with a variable\n32-bit right shift whose amount is formed from the effective address. That\ndatapath feeds writeback and the next-cycle forwarding path for dependent\nloads, so a synthesized barrel-shifter-style cone may limit Fmax even\nthough only four byte lanes are legal.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n{\"motivation\":\"The MEM stage currently extracts byte and halfword loads with a variable\\n32-bit right shift whose amount is formed from the effective address. That\\ndatapath feeds writeback and the next-cycle forwarding path for dependent\\nloads, so a synthesized barrel-shifter-style cone may limit Fmax even\\nthough only four byte lanes are legal.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Replace the variable load shift with an explicit four-way byte-lane mux and\\na two-way halfword-lane mux, and derive the byte masks from constant lookup\\ncases. Keep word loads, sign/zero extension, misalignment traps, dmem\\nhandshaking, and all RVFI memory fields unchanged. The narrower constant\\nlane network should reduce LUT depth and routing on the load-to-writeback\\npath without changing instruction timing or retirement behavior.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement explicit address-indexed load lane muxes and constant byte-mask cases, preserving exact RV32I load semantics, traps, handshakes, and RVFI fields.\"}]}\nCHANGES:\nrtl/mem_stage.sv :: Implement explicit address-indexed load lane muxes and constant byte-mask cases, preserving exact RV32I load semantics, traps, handshakes, and RVFI fields.\nNOTES:\n\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 Source-use-aware load-use interlock\nMOTIVATION:\nThe load-use detector currently compares a load destination against both\nrs1 and rs2 bit fields of every following instruction, even when that\ninstruction does not read one or either field. Immediate, upper-immediate,\njump, fence, and system encodings can therefore consume a needless bubble\nin CoreMark. Removing these false hazards improves retired instructions per\ncycle without changing the five-stage timing or the external bus contract.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n{\"motivation\":\"The load-use detector currently compares a load destination against both\\nrs1 and rs2 bit fields of every following instruction, even when that\\ninstruction does not read one or either field. Immediate, upper-immediate,\\njump, fence, and system encodings can therefore consume a needless bubble\\nin CoreMark. Removing these false hazards improves retired instructions per\\ncycle without changing the five-stage timing or the external bus contract.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Derive precise source-use bits for the IF/ID instruction from the existing\\nlegal RV32IM decode and feed them into the hazard unit. Stall for load-use\\nonly when the load rd is nonzero and matches a genuinely read rs1 or rs2;\\npreserve the current one-cycle interlock for R-type, branch, store, and\\naddress-generating consumers, along with all redirect, dmem-stall, x0, trap,\\nand RVFI ordering behavior. This should eliminate false bubbles while\\nleaving the optimized ALU and the common execute datapath unchanged.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Expose decode-derived use_rs1/use_rs2 signals for the current IF/ID instruction, asserting them only for valid RV32IM consumers.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Gate load-use comparisons with the source-use signals while retaining exact load, x0, redirect, instruction-stall, and data-stall precedence.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the ID-stage source-use metadata into hazard detection without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}]}\nCHANGES:\nrtl/id_stage.sv :: Expose decode-derived use_rs1/use_rs2 signals for the current IF/ID instruction, asserting them only for valid RV32IM consumers.\nrtl/hazard_unit.sv :: Gate load-use comparisons with the source-use signals while retaining exact load, x0, redirect, instruction-stall, and data-stall precedence.\nrtl/core.sv :: Wire the ID-stage source-use metadata into hazard detection without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\nNOTES:\n\n\n### hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 Consumer-specific EX operand mux factoring\nMOTIVATION:\nThe optimized core still builds fully forwarded rs1/rs2 operands before\napplying the EX-stage PC/immediate selection. For common ADDI and memory\naddress operations, that leaves an unnecessary forwarding mux on the ALU\ninput even though the instruction cannot use the forwarded value there.\nThe prior ALU optimization improved Fmax without changing IPC, so removing\nanother level of common execute-path muxing is a promising low-risk follow-up.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n{\"motivation\":\"The optimized core still builds fully forwarded rs1/rs2 operands before\\napplying the EX-stage PC/immediate selection. For common ADDI and memory\\naddress operations, that leaves an unnecessary forwarding mux on the ALU\\ninput even though the instruction cannot use the forwarded value there.\\nThe prior ALU optimization improved Fmax without changing IPC, so removing\\nanother level of common execute-path muxing is a promising low-risk follow-up.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Factor the EX operand network by consumer: select the immediate directly as\\nthe ALU B operand when alu_src is set, and select PC directly as the ALU A\\noperand for AUIPC, while retaining the existing freshest-writer forwarding\\nmuxes for branch comparisons, stores, JALR, and RVFI source values. This\\nshould shorten the dominant address-generation and integer ALU paths and\\nreduce forwarding-control fanout without adding latency, changing hazards,\\nor changing any architectural result or retirement metadata.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Refactor ALU operand selection so immediate and PC consumers bypass irrelevant forwarding mux levels while branches, stores, JALR, and RVFI retain exact forwarded operands.\"}]}\nCHANGES:\nrtl/ex_stage.sv :: Refactor ALU operand selection so immediate and PC consumers bypass irrelevant forwarding mux levels while branches, stores, JALR, and RVFI retain exact forwarded operands.\nNOTES:\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
