7520,\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Fuse branch, JAL, and JALR target formation into one gated PC/rs1 plus immediate adder while preserving redirect and misalignment-trap semantics.\"}],\"lesson\":\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.33%): Sharing branch/JAL/JALR target arithmetic through a control-gated `target_base` mux and common `target_sum` can worsen CoreMark when the added EX selection and fanout cost more than the removed parallel adders, so arithmetic sharing must be evaluated against its control-path depth.\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\",\"title\":\"Direct operand bypass muxing\",\"outcome\":\"broken\",\"fitness\":null,\"delta_pct\":null,\"fmax_mhz\":null,\"lut4\":null,\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Replace two-bit forwarding selects with source-use-gated EX/MEM and MEM/WB hit/data bypass signals, preserving freshest-writer priority and x0 suppression.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Consume the direct priority bypass values for ALU, branch, and store operands, minimizing the operand mux depth while retaining exact forwarding behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire ID/EX operand values and decoded source-use controls into the direct bypass network without changing the pipeline or RVFI contract.\"}],\"lesson\":\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Replacing encoded forwarding selects with direct EX/MEM/MEM/WB bypass hits must preserve freshest-writer priority and the forwarded values latched into RVFI source fields, or dependent instructions can expose stale registers and fail `reg_ch0`.\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-001-r5s0\",\"title\":\"Explicit load lane extraction\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":370.39,\"delta_pct\":-8.97,\"fmax_mhz\":166.36,\"lut4\":7488,\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement explicit address-indexed load lane muxes and constant byte-mask cases, preserving exact RV32I load semantics, traps, handshakes, and RVFI fields.\"}],\"lesson\":\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -8.97%): Replacing variable load shifts with explicit lane-case muxes did not pay off in CoreMark, showing that added MEM-stage selection and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings when the existing shift is already synthesized efficiently.\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-001-r6s0\",\"title\":\"Source-use-aware load-use interlock\",\"outcome\":\"broken\",\"fitness\":null,\"delta_pct\":null,\"fmax_mhz\":null,\"lut4\":null,\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Expose decode-derived use_rs1/use_rs2 signals for the current IF/ID instruction, asserting them only for valid RV32IM consumers.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Gate load-use comparisons with the source-use signals while retaining exact load, x0, redirect, instruction-stall, and data-stall precedence.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the ID-stage source-use metadata into hazard detection without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}],\"lesson\":\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `use_rs1`/`use_rs2` masks make load-use interlocks correctness-critical—any mismatch with the legal instruction decode or IF/ID timing can miss a required bubble and corrupt architectural register state, which `reg_ch0` catches.\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-001-r7s0\",\"title\":\"Consumer-specific EX operand mux factoring\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":368.01,\"delta_pct\":-9.55,\"fmax_mhz\":165.29,\"lut4\":7611,\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Refactor ALU operand selection so immediate and PC consumers bypass irrelevant forwarding mux levels while branches, stores, JALR, and RVFI retain exact forwarded operands.\"}],\"lesson\":\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.55%): Consumer-specific EX operand factoring can regress CoreMark when it duplicates the `fwd_rs1_sel`/`fwd_rs2_sel` mux trees for ALU inputs alongside canonical forwarded operands, so shortening nominal immediate/PC paths must be weighed against added mux replication and routing fanout.\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-001-r8s0\",\"title\":\"Predecoded class/sub-op ALU control\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":318.29,\"delta_pct\":-21.77,\"fmax_mhz\":142.96,\"lut4\":7468,\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":2,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add compact predecoded ALU class/sub-operation fields to the pipeline control bundle while retaining all existing trap and memory controls.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/decoder.sv\",\"description\":\"Generate the explicit M-class and compact ALU sub-operation controls only inside validated RV32IM opcode/funct matches, preserving strict illegal defaults.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch the predecoded ALU class/sub-operation controls into ID/EX without changing operand, hazard, or RVFI metadata timing.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/alu.sv\",\"description\":\"Use the predecoded class/sub-operation selects to bypass 5-bit range/equality decoding on the common RV32I path while preserving real M arithmetic and ALTOPS semantics.\"}],\"lesson\":\"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (regression, -21.77%): Predecoding ALU class/sub-operation fields did not pay off when the added ID/EX control fields and fanout outweighed removing EX range comparisons, showing that narrower control encoding helps only if it also reduces physical routing on the common CoreMark path.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n -i \"critical path|timing|fanout|mux|decode|forward|hazard|register|memory|ALU|adder|shift|branch\" cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"1:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.87%): Separating the RV32I fast-path mux from gated M-extension multiply/divide logic, while deriving signed multiply high halves from one unsigned product, can substantially improve CoreMark by removing unconditional arithmetic fanout from ordinary integer and address-generation operations.\n3:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.25%): A fetch-time BTFNT predictor can lose badly when `imem_data` branch decode, immediate formation, target addition, and prediction metadata extend the PC/IF critical path and recovery overhead enough to outweigh saved backward-loop redirects in CoreMark.\n5:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.33%): Sharing branch/JAL/JALR target arithmetic through a control-gated `target_base` mux and common `target_sum` can worsen CoreMark when the added EX selection and fanout cost more than the removed parallel adders, so arithmetic sharing must be evaluated against its control-path depth.\n6:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): ID-stage branch and jump redirects must latch resolved next-PC and misalignment-trap metadata with the instruction through ID/EX to retirement, because advancing fetch without keeping RVFI `pc_wdata` aligned can fail `pc_fwd` even when instruction checks pass.\n7:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 (regression, -63.70%): Decode-stage RAS prediction can lose badly when speculative push/pop checkpoints, recovery control, and carried predicted-PC metadata add ID/EX/EX muxing and fanout that cost more than the saved JALR return flushes in CoreMark.\n8:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Replacing encoded forwarding selects with direct EX/MEM/MEM/WB bypass hits must preserve freshest-writer priority and the forwarded values latched into RVFI source fields, or dependent instructions can expose stale registers and fail `reg_ch0`.\n9:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -7.94%): On a single-ported dmem, a one-entry store buffer can lose to its own store-priority arbitration, address-conflict checks, and MEM-stage load-forward path, whose control and conservative ordering stalls outweigh the store bubbles they hide in CoreMark.\n10:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -8.97%): Replacing variable load shifts with explicit lane-case muxes did not pay off in CoreMark, showing that added MEM-stage selection and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings when the existing shift is already synthesized efficiently.\n11:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (regression, -53.64%): Retiming EX into EX1/EX2 made dependency, redirect, and dmem-stall interlocks pay extra latency on common in-order paths, so CoreMark IPC loss overwhelmed any Fmax benefit; deeper pipelines need latency-aware bypassing without serializing ALU chains.\n13:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `use_rs1`/`use_rs2` masks make load-use interlocks correctness-critical—any mismatch with the legal instruction decode or IF/ID timing can miss a required bubble and corrupt architectural register state, which `reg_ch0` catches.\n14:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry ALU completion skid must separate memory completion, skid capture/drain, and EX/MEM clearing while preserving the single MEM/WB retirement slot and age ordering, or `dmemReady` transitions can corrupt CoreMark execution.\n16:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.55%): Consumer-specific EX operand factoring can regress CoreMark when it duplicates the `fwd_rs1_sel`/`fwd_rs2_sel` mux trees for ALU inputs alongside canonical forwarded operands, so shortening nominal immediate/PC paths must be weighed against added mux replication and routing fanout.\n17:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -1.64%): Stage-local micro-op typedefs do not guarantee a timing win when EX/MEM still carries broad retirement metadata and the split adds payload repacking and routing, so control partitioning must reduce physical fanout rather than only reorganize fields.\n19:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (regression, -21.77%): Predecoding ALU class/sub-operation fields did not pay off when the added ID/EX control fields and fanout outweighed removing EX range comparisons, showing that narrower control encoding helps only if it also reduces physical routing on the common CoreMark path.\n20:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (regression, -6.53%): Reset-masked replicated LUTRAM register files can regress when two full data banks plus per-read valid-bit checks add replication and operand-path control, so distributed-RAM inference must be measured against the compact resettable flop baseline rather than assumed to reduce routing.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
