{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r11s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Small direct-mapped write-through data cache\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Every normal load and store currently reaches the external dmem port, so\n  the benchmark's random dmemReady stalls freeze the entire in-order pipe.\n  CoreMark repeatedly accesses a small working set, and the FPGA has block\n  RAM resources that can hold a modest cache without adding a new pipeline\n  stage or speculative control state.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 2 KiB direct-mapped write-through data cache with four-word lines for\n  the normal RAM range only. A load hit returns the cached word locally and\n  avoids the external dmem handshake; a miss holds the oldest request and\n  refills the line in order before releasing the pipeline. Stores remain\n  write-through, update a valid cache line on a hit, and use no-write-allocate\n  on a miss. Explicitly bypass the cache for UART/marker MMIO and perform\n  alignment checks before lookup, while preserving the existing MEM/WB and\n  RVFI memory fields. Repeated CoreMark accesses should therefore avoid many\n  random dmem stalls while retirement remains single-issue and in order.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/data_cache.sv\n    description: \"Implement a reset-safe direct-mapped four-word-line write-through cache for normal RAM, with tag/valid storage, hit response, serialized miss refill, store masks, and explicit MMIO bypass.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Place the cache between EX/MEM and the existing dmem/MEM-WB boundary, holding one oldest request through refill or write-through completion while preserving alignment traps, byte lanes, load extension, and RVFI raw-memory reporting.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the cache request/response and busy signals into the existing hazard and dmem-stall control so cache hits flow at one per cycle and misses cannot duplicate, reorder, or double-retire an instruction.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
