{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.87%): Separating the RV32I fast-path mux from gated M-extension multiply/divide logic, while deriving signed multiply high halves from one unsigned product, can substantially improve CoreMark by removing unconditional arithmetic fanout from ordinary integer and address-generation operations.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 (regression, +7.15%): A small prefetch FIFO is not automatically beneficial when EX redirects flush every queued instruction and FIFO-empty refill inserts ID bubbles, so redirect frequency and refill cost can outweigh hiding random imem backpressure in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.25%): A fetch-time BTFNT predictor can lose badly when `imem_data` branch decode, immediate formation, target addition, and prediction metadata extend the PC/IF critical path and recovery overhead enough to outweigh saved backward-loop redirects in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A PC-indexed BTB must carry prediction-valid/next-PC metadata atomically with the fetched instruction and reconcile it with EX `resolved_next_pc` and RVFI `pc_wdata`, or redirect recovery can break `pc_fwd` even when instruction checks pass.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.33%): Sharing branch/JAL/JALR target arithmetic through a control-gated `target_base` mux and common `target_sum` can worsen CoreMark when the added EX selection and fanout cost more than the removed parallel adders, so arithmetic sharing must be evaluated against its control-path depth.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): ID-stage branch and jump redirects must latch resolved next-PC and misalignment-trap metadata with the instruction through ID/EX to retirement, because advancing fetch without keeping RVFI `pc_wdata` aligned can fail `pc_fwd` even when instruction checks pass.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 (regression, -63.70%): Decode-stage RAS prediction can lose badly when speculative push/pop checkpoints, recovery control, and carried predicted-PC metadata add ID/EX/EX muxing and fanout that cost more than the saved JALR return flushes in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Replacing encoded forwarding selects with direct EX/MEM/MEM/WB bypass hits must preserve freshest-writer priority and the forwarded values latched into RVFI source fields, or dependent instructions can expose stale registers and fail `reg_ch0`.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -7.94%): On a single-ported dmem, a one-entry store buffer can lose to its own store-priority arbitration, address-conflict checks, and MEM-stage load-forward path, whose control and conservative ordering stalls outweigh the store bubbles they hide in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -8.97%): Replacing variable load shifts with explicit lane-case muxes did not pay off in CoreMark, showing that added MEM-stage selection and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings when the existing shift is already synthesized efficiently.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (regression, -53.64%): Retiming EX into EX1/EX2 made dependency, redirect, and dmem-stall interlocks pay extra latency on common in-order paths, so CoreMark IPC loss overwhelmed any Fmax benefit; deeper pipelines need latency-aware bypassing without serializing ALU chains.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (regression, -24.58%): Even confidence-gating a two-entry tagged BTB can regress CoreMark when every fetched instruction carries `predicted_valid`/`predicted_next_pc` through ID/EX and pays IF next-PC selection plus EX resolved-next-PC comparison and flush-repair overhead for a small number of saved loop redirects.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `use_rs1`/`use_rs2` masks make load-use interlocks correctness-critical—any mismatch with the legal instruction decode or IF/ID timing can miss a required bubble and corrupt architectural register state, which `reg_ch0` catches.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry ALU completion skid must separate memory completion, skid capture/drain, and EX/MEM clearing while preserving the single MEM/WB retirement slot and age ordering, or `dmemReady` transitions can corrupt CoreMark execution.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -1.34%): Even a one-entry exact-PC target table with only a one-bit pipeline token can regress CoreMark when IF target selection plus EX re-lookup/agreement and miss-repair redirects cost more than the repeatedly taken loop flushes it removes.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.55%): Consumer-specific EX operand factoring can regress CoreMark when it duplicates the `fwd_rs1_sel`/`fwd_rs2_sel` mux trees for ALU inputs alongside canonical forwarded operands, so shortening nominal immediate/PC paths must be weighed against added mux replication and routing fanout.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -1.64%): Stage-local micro-op typedefs do not guarantee a timing win when EX/MEM still carries broad retirement metadata and the split adds payload repacking and routing, so control partitioning must reduce physical fanout rather than only reorganize fields.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, -6.48%): Even a zero-state aligned-JAL predictor can regress CoreMark when IF-side J-immediate formation and target addition lengthen the fetch path and the carried prediction token plus EX agreement check add recovery/control overhead that outweighs saved JAL flushes.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (regression, -21.77%): Predecoding ALU class/sub-operation fields did not pay off when the added ID/EX control fields and fanout outweighed removing EX range comparisons, showing that narrower control encoding helps only if it also reduces physical routing on the common CoreMark path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (regression, -6.53%): Reset-masked replicated LUTRAM register files can regress when two full data banks plus per-read valid-bit checks add replication and operand-path control, so distributed-RAM inference must be measured against the compact resettable flop baseline rather than assumed to reduce routing.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (regression, -55.91%): Bit-factoring M-operation and multiply/divide predicates can regress even as it removes relational compares, showing that decode Boolean simplification is not a proxy for a shorter synthesized control-to-result path or lower fanout.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): An iterative EX divider must hold one DIV/REM transaction's operands, instruction metadata, and RVFI/forwarding age through start/busy/done and release younger pipeline state only after completion, or all four M-operation checks and `reg_ch0` can fail together.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (broken, n/a): An epoch-qualified loop-prediction token is unsafe unless EX compares its epoch with the live predictor entry; checking only `loop_pred_entry_valid` and target can suppress a required redirect after replacement or flush and violate RVFI `pc_fwd`.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (improvement, +1.35%): A single 33-bit EX subtract with zero, borrow, and overflow-corrected signed-less-than flags can modestly improve CoreMark by sharing BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branch cones without adding pipeline latency.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -39.27%): Making a one-entry loop target immutable with the instruction does not make prediction profitable when every hit still drives an IF next-PC mux and IF/ID/EX token path, while alias or stale hits require EX repair redirects that outweigh saved CoreMark loop flushes.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r11s0 (regression, -17.20%): Reusing ALU_SUB flags for branches regressed CoreMark, showing that crossing the ALU/EX interface with zero, borrow, and signed-less-than flag fanout can lengthen the branch-control path even when the subtract arithmetic is textually shared.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r11s1 (broken, n/a): A cache integration that instantiates `data_cache` without adding its module to the `rtl/*.sv` build inputs is structurally incomplete and fails cosim before cache performance can be measured.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r11s2 (broken, n/a): A fetch-side return hint must be canceled or repaired for a resolved misaligned JALR, because EX suppressing the trap redirect after IF already advanced to the predicted target violates RVFI `pc_fwd`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
