{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r6s1.yaml && git diff --check -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r6s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Constrained ALU completion skid during data stalls\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  A dmemReady stall currently freezes the entire five-stage machine even when\n  the instruction behind the blocked load or store is an independent integer\n  operation. The earlier full elastic-memory queue was too broad and failed\n  liveness, while a store-only buffer added arbitration and ordering cost. A\n  much narrower completion structure can recover some of the lost overlap\n  without speculating or reordering any memory operation.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry in-order completion skid between EX and MEM that accepts only\n  younger, non-memory, non-control ALU instructions while an older EX/MEM load\n  or store waits for io_dmemReady. Keep the blocked memory operation as the\n  retirement head, forbid younger loads, stores, branches, jumps, and illegal\n  operations from entering the skid, and stop the front end once the skid is\n  full. After the memory completes, drain the skid entry into the normal\n  MEM/WB path before accepting another result. Add explicit forwarding from\n  the skid entry and preserve age ordering, precise redirects, RVFI source\n  values, and register writeback. This limited bypass should hide short random\n  dmem stalls with modest area and control overhead while avoiding the timing\n  and correctness risks of a general completion queue.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Define a compact completion-skid payload and valid/age metadata for a younger ALU result while retaining the existing RVFI payload fields.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Integrate the one-entry ALU skid between EX and MEM, select the oldest completed result for writeback, and connect skid forwarding without changing the single-channel RVFI contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose a sideband candidate for non-memory, non-control ALU instructions and prevent skid capture of operations that could redirect, trap, or access memory.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add ordered drain/capture behavior so a blocked older data request remains the retirement head and the younger ALU completion reaches MEM/WB only after it.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace unconditional dmem-stall freezing with skid-full and dependency-aware backpressure, while keeping memory ordering, load-use hazards, redirect flushes, and forward progress precise.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Give the completion skid highest forwarding priority for dependent ALU operands, with x0 and valid gating and older EX/MEM/MEM/WB priority preserved when the skid is absent.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
