{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r8s1.yaml && test \\\"\"'$(rg -c '\"'\"'^id: hyp-20260710-002-r8s1$'\"' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r8s1.yaml)\\\" -eq 1\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-002-r8s1\ntitle: \"Reset-masked replicated LUTRAM register file\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current two-read, one-write register file is implemented as a reset-cleared\n  32x32 flop array with two asynchronous read mux networks. That structure adds\n  substantial FPGA routing and fanout on the ID-to-EX operand path even though\n  the architectural register state is only 1024 bits. Tang Nano LUT resources\n  can pack small replicated memories more efficiently than a broad resettable\n  flop bank.\nhypothesis: |\n  Rebuild the register file as two replicated 32-entry distributed-RAM banks,\n  one dedicated to each asynchronous read port, with a shared synchronous write\n  update. Replace data-array reset writes with a reset-cleared per-register valid\n  bitmap; reads of invalid entries and x0 return zero, while writes set the\n  corresponding valid bit. Retain the existing same-cycle write-first bypass\n  and x0 write suppression. This should preserve exact architectural and RVFI\n  behavior while shortening register-file routing, reducing reset fanout, and\n  improving routed Fmax without changing pipeline latency or CoreMark IPC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Replace the reset-cleared flop array with two write-synchronized distributed-RAM read banks plus a reset-valid bitmap, preserving zero reads, x0 behavior, and write-first bypass semantics.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
