{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n -i 'predict|btb|branch|loop|ras|target|jump|return' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 120\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.87%): Separating the RV32I fast-path mux from gated M-extension multiply/divide logic, while deriving signed multiply high halves from one unsigned product, can substantially improve CoreMark by removing unconditional arithmetic fanout from ordinary integer and address-generation operations.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 (regression, +7.15%): A small prefetch FIFO is not automatically beneficial when EX redirects flush every queued instruction and FIFO-empty refill inserts ID bubbles, so redirect frequency and refill cost can outweigh hiding random imem backpressure in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.25%): A fetch-time BTFNT predictor can lose badly when `imem_data` branch decode, immediate formation, target addition, and prediction metadata extend the PC/IF critical path and recovery overhead enough to outweigh saved backward-loop redirects in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A PC-indexed BTB must carry prediction-valid/next-PC metadata atomically with the fetched instruction and reconcile it with EX `resolved_next_pc` and RVFI `pc_wdata`, or redirect recovery can break `pc_fwd` even when instruction checks pass.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -8.33%): Sharing branch/JAL/JALR target arithmetic through a control-gated `target_base` mux and common `target_sum` can worsen CoreMark when the added EX selection and fanout cost more than the removed parallel adders, so arithmetic sharing must be evaluated against its control-path depth.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): ID-stage branch and jump redirects must latch resolved next-PC and misalignment-trap metadata with the instruction through ID/EX to retirement, because advancing fetch without keeping RVFI `pc_wdata` aligned can fail `pc_fwd` even when instruction checks pass.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 (regression, -63.70%): Decode-stage RAS prediction can lose badly when speculative push/pop checkpoints, recovery control, and carried predicted-PC metadata add ID/EX/EX muxing and fanout that cost more than the saved JALR return flushes in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (broken, n/a): Replacing encoded forwarding selects with direct EX/MEM/MEM/WB bypass hits must preserve freshest-writer priority and the forwarded values latched into RVFI source fields, or dependent instructions can expose stale registers and fail `reg_ch0`.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (regression, -7.94%): On a single-ported dmem, a one-entry store buffer can lose to its own store-priority arbitration, address-conflict checks, and MEM-stage load-forward path, whose control and conservative ordering stalls outweigh the store bubbles they hide in CoreMark.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -8.97%): Replacing variable load shifts with explicit lane-case muxes did not pay off in CoreMark, showing that added MEM-stage selection and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings when the existing shift is already synthesized efficiently.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (regression, -53.64%): Retiming EX into EX1/EX2 made dependency, redirect, and dmem-stall interlocks pay extra latency on common in-order paths, so CoreMark IPC loss overwhelmed any Fmax benefit; deeper pipelines need latency-aware bypassing without serializing ALU chains.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (regression, -24.58%): Even confidence-gating a two-entry tagged BTB can regress CoreMark when every fetched instruction carries `predicted_valid`/`predicted_next_pc` through ID/EX and pays IF next-PC selection plus EX resolved-next-PC comparison and flush-repair overhead for a small number of saved loop redirects.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-derived `use_rs1`/`use_rs2` masks make load-use interlocks correctness-critical—any mismatch with the legal instruction decode or IF/ID timing can miss a required bubble and corrupt architectural register state, which `reg_ch0` catches.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry ALU completion skid must separate memory completion, skid capture/drain, and EX/MEM clearing while preserving the single MEM/WB retirement slot and age ordering, or `dmemReady` transitions can corrupt CoreMark execution.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -1.34%): Even a one-entry exact-PC target table with only a one-bit pipeline token can regress CoreMark when IF target selection plus EX re-lookup/agreement and miss-repair redirects cost more than the repeatedly taken loop flushes it removes.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.55%): Consumer-specific EX operand factoring can regress CoreMark when it duplicates the `fwd_rs1_sel`/`fwd_rs2_sel` mux trees for ALU inputs alongside canonical forwarded operands, so shortening nominal immediate/PC paths must be weighed against added mux replication and routing fanout.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (regression, -1.64%): Stage-local micro-op typedefs do not guarantee a timing win when EX/MEM still carries broad retirement metadata and the split adds payload repacking and routing, so control partitioning must reduce physical fanout rather than only reorganize fields.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, -6.48%): Even a zero-state aligned-JAL predictor can regress CoreMark when IF-side J-immediate formation and target addition lengthen the fetch path and the carried prediction token plus EX agreement check add recovery/control overhead that outweighs saved JAL flushes.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (regression, -21.77%): Predecoding ALU class/sub-operation fields did not pay off when the added ID/EX control fields and fanout outweighed removing EX range comparisons, showing that narrower control encoding helps only if it also reduces physical routing on the common CoreMark path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (regression, -6.53%): Reset-masked replicated LUTRAM register files can regress when two full data banks plus per-read valid-bit checks add replication and operand-path control, so distributed-RAM inference must be measured against the compact resettable flop baseline rather than assumed to reduce routing.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (regression, -55.91%): Bit-factoring M-operation and multiply/divide predicates can regress even as it removes relational compares, showing that decode Boolean simplification is not a proxy for a shorter synthesized control-to-result path or lower fanout.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): An iterative EX divider must hold one DIV/REM transaction's operands, instruction metadata, and RVFI/forwarding age through start/busy/done and release younger pipeline state only after completion, or all four M-operation checks and `reg_ch0` can fail together.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (broken, n/a): An epoch-qualified loop-prediction token is unsafe unless EX compares its epoch with the live predictor entry; checking only `loop_pred_entry_valid` and target can suppress a required redirect after replacement or flush and violate RVFI `pc_fwd`.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (improvement, +1.35%): A single 33-bit EX subtract with zero, borrow, and overflow-corrected signed-less-than flags can modestly improve CoreMark by sharing BEQ/BNE and signed/unsigned relational branch cones without adding pipeline latency.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -39.27%): Making a one-entry loop target immutable with the instruction does not make prediction profitable when every hit still drives an IF next-PC mux and IF/ID/EX token path, while alias or stale hits require EX repair redirects that outweigh saved CoreMark loop flushes.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r11s0 (regression, -17.20%): Reusing ALU_SUB flags for branches regressed CoreMark, showing that crossing the ALU/EX interface with zero, borrow, and signed-less-than flag fanout can lengthen the branch-control path even when the subtract arithmetic is textually shared.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r11s1 (broken, n/a): A cache integration that instantiates `data_cache` without adding its module to the `rtl/*.sv` build inputs is structurally incomplete and fails cosim before cache performance can be measured.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r11s2 (broken, n/a): A fetch-side return hint must be canceled or repaired for a resolved misaligned JALR, because EX suppressing the trap redirect after IF already advanced to the predicted target violates RVFI `pc_fwd`.\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decoupled instruction prefetch FIFO\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage stalls the entire five-stage pipeline whenever\\nio_imemReady is low, so the benchmark's random instruction-bus stalls\\ndirectly reduce retired instructions per cycle. CoreMark has enough\\nstraight-line execution between redirects for a small queue to hide many\\nof those bubbles without changing the external bus contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage stalls the entire five-stage pipeline whenever\\nio_imemReady is low, so the benchmark's random instruction-bus stalls\\ndirectly reduce retired instructions per cycle. CoreMark has enough\\nstraight-line execution between redirects for a small queue to hide many\\nof those bubbles without changing the external bus contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a four-entry tagged instruction prefetch FIFO between imem and ID.\\nKeep a fetch address generator filling the FIFO whenever io_imemReady is\\nhigh, let ID consume queued instructions independently of transient fetch\\nbackpressure, and flush the queue plus restart its fetch pointer on an EX\\nredirect. Preserve the existing in-order valid/PC flow and make the queue\\nimpose backpressure only when empty, so branch recovery and RVFI retirement\\nremain architecturally identical while instruction stalls are overlapped.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single-PC fetch hold behavior with a four-entry tagged instruction prefetch FIFO, including ready-aware fill, dequeue, redirect flush, and precise PC/valid output.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconnect front-end stall and flush handling so downstream hazards only stop FIFO consumption when necessary, while imem backpressure does not freeze already-buffered instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate instruction-queue-empty backpressure from imemReady stalls and retain redirect/load-use/dmem stall precedence without allowing duplicate or skipped fetches.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single-PC fetch hold behavior with a four-entry tagged instruction prefetch FIFO, including ready-aware fill, dequeue, redirect flush, and precise PC/valid output.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconnect front-end stall and flush handling so downstream hazards only stop FIFO consumption when necessary, while imem backpressure does not freeze already-buffered instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate instruction-queue-empty backpressure from imemReady stalls and retain redirect/load-use/dmem stall precedence without allowing duplicate or skipped fetches.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 303.05, \"delta_pct\": 7.15, \"vs_baseline\": 7.15, \"fmax_mhz\": 142.35, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4697199, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9969, \"ff\": 1966, \"seeds\": [134.83, 142.86, 142.35], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:11:33.613459Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 (regression, +7.15%): A small prefetch FIFO is not automatically beneficial when EX redirects flush every queued instruction and FIFO-empty refill inserts ID bubbles, so redirect frequency and refill cost can outweigh hiding random imem backpressure in CoreMark.\"}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Fetch-time backward-taken branch prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage bench core resolves every conditional branch in EX and\\notherwise fetches sequentially, so taken loop branches pay a redirect and\\nflush penalty. CoreMark contains many tight backward loops, while the FPGA\\nimplementation has little LUT headroom for a sizeable BTB or history table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage bench core resolves every conditional branch in EX and\\notherwise fetches sequentially, so taken loop branches pay a redirect and\\nflush penalty. CoreMark contains many tight backward loops, while the FPGA\\nimplementation has little LUT headroom for a sizeable BTB or history table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a low-area static BTFNT predictor in IF: recognize conditional branch\\nopcodes from the fetched instruction, predict negative-offset branches taken,\\nand form their immediate target during fetch. Carry prediction metadata with\\nthe instruction and make EX redirect only when the predicted next PC differs\\nfrom the resolved next PC, including the predicted-taken/actually-not-taken\\nloop-exit case. This should remove most taken-loop bubbles without adding\\npredictor state or weakening architectural recovery and RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted-next-PC and prediction-valid metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement fetch-time BTFNT recognition and direct B-immediate target generation, while preserving stall and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pipeline the branch prediction metadata alongside the decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare predicted and resolved branch next PCs, redirect on misprediction, and retain misalignment/trap recovery semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the prediction-aware redirect and flush behavior through the existing pipeline control path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted-next-PC and prediction-valid metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement fetch-time BTFNT recognition and direct B-immediate target generation, while preserving stall and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pipeline the branch prediction metadata alongside the decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare predicted and resolved branch next PCs, redirect on misprediction, and retain misalignment/trap recovery semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the prediction-aware redirect and flush behavior through the existing pipeline control path.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 348.58, \"delta_pct\": 23.25, \"vs_baseline\": 23.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 150.22, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4309463, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9985, \"ff\": 1900, \"seeds\": [150.4, 150.22, 141.42], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:03:36.714593Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.25%): A fetch-time BTFNT predictor can lose badly when `imem_data` branch decode, immediate formation, target addition, and prediction metadata extend the PC/IF critical path and recovery overhead enough to outweigh saved backward-loop redirects in CoreMark.\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic in-order data-memory completion queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM-stage handshake freezes PC, ID/EX, and EX/MEM whenever\\nio_dmemReady is low, so a transient data-bus stall blocks unrelated ALU\\ninstructions and directly lowers CoreMark instructions per cycle. The\\nrecent fetch FIFO result also shows that buffering only the front end is\\ninsufficient when the dominant stall is downstream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM-stage handshake freezes PC, ID/EX, and EX/MEM whenever\\nio_dmemReady is low, so a transient data-bus stall blocks unrelated ALU\\ninstructions and directly lowers CoreMark instructions per cycle. The\\nrecent fetch FIFO result also shows that buffering only the front end is\\ninsufficient when the dominant stall is downstream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the memory boundary as a small elastic in-order queue with a\\ndedicated completion/retirement head. Allow younger independent ALU\\ninstructions to execute into queue entries while an older load or store\\nwaits for io_dmemReady, but prevent younger memory operations from passing\\nan older memory operation and hold dependent consumers until the load data\\nis available. Retire only the queue head, preserving RVFI order, precise\\ntraps, register writeback, and architectural memory ordering. This should\\noverlap random dmem backpressure with useful integer work without adding a\\nfetch-time critical path or changing the external bus contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the metadata needed for elastic memory/completion entries, including age, completion, and deferred load/store state while preserving the existing RVFI payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single blocking MEM/WB boundary with a small in-order memory/completion queue that holds stalled data requests, accepts younger non-memory results, and exposes only the oldest completed entry to writeback and RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate queue-full, older-memory-order, and load-result dependency stalls instead of freezing the complete pipeline on every dmemReady low cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire elastic queue ready/valid flow through EX, MEM, WB, and retirement so dmem backpressure overlaps independent execution while redirects and RVFI remain precise.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the metadata needed for elastic memory/completion entries, including age, completion, and deferred load/store state while preserving the existing RVFI payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single blocking MEM/WB boundary with a small in-order memory/completion queue that holds stalled data requests, accepts younger non-memory results, and exposes only the oldest completed entry to writeback and RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate queue-full, older-memory-order, and load-result dependency stalls instead of freezing the complete pipeline on every dmemReady low cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire elastic queue ready/valid flow through EX, MEM, WB, and retirement so dmem backpressure overlaps independen