er operators still synthesize into a\\nlarge variable-division cone in the execute stage. That cone can limit the\\nFPGA clock even when CoreMark spends most cycles on integer and address\\ngeneration work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of the combinational ALU into a dedicated\\niterative execute-side unit with a registered request, busy state, and\\nresult completion. Stall only the in-order pipeline while an M divide is\\nactive, forward the completed result through the existing writeback path,\\nand retain the exact RV32M divide-by-zero and signed-overflow semantics.\\nRemoving the / and % cones from the normal EX result path should raise Fmax;\\nthe extra latency should have limited CoreMark impact because divide and\\nremainder instructions are uncommon relative to the common integer path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU arithmetic from the common ALU result path and expose only the fast integer and multiply operations to EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the registered iterative divide/remainder execution state, completion result, and precise operand/control capture while preserving forwarding and misalignment/trap metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate a divide-unit busy/hold interlock that freezes younger instructions without duplicating or retiring the in-flight divide, while retaining existing load-use, redirect, and dmem-stall precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divide request, busy, and completion signals through the pipeline so DIV/REM retire once in order through the existing single RVFI channel.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU arit