{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"\nhyp-20260710-003-r1s2\nAdd a low-area static BTFNT predictor in IF: recognize conditional branch\nopcodes from the fetched instruction, predict negative-offset branches taken,\nand form their immediate target during fetch. Carry prediction metadata with\nthe instruction and make EX redirect only when the predicted next PC differs\nfrom the resolved next PC, including the predicted-taken/actually-not-taken\nloop-exit case. This should remove most taken-loop bubbles without adding\npredictor state or weakening architectural recovery and RVFI behavior.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r2s2\nAdd a tiny direct-mapped BTB with PC tags, cached branch targets, and a\ntwo-bit taken counter. A tagged hit in IF predicts the cached target using\nonly the current PC, keeping opcode/funct/immediate decode out of the IF\ncritical path. Carry the predicted next PC with the instruction; in EX,\ncompare it with the resolved branch next PC and redirect only on a\nmisprediction. Train the BTB from resolved conditional branches, saturating\ntoward taken or not-taken, while retaining precise flush, trap, RVFI, and\nload/store stall behavior. This should remove repeated taken-loop redirect\nbubbles without repeating the timing-costly fetch-time BTFNT logic.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r3s2\nAdd a small speculative return-address stack and perform return prediction\nin ID for canonical JALR returns (x1/x5 link register, zero immediate).\nTrack call pushes and return pops with a shadow/speculative stack, carry an\nexplicit predicted-next-PC and prediction-valid bit through the pipeline,\nand compare the prediction with EX's resolved next PC. On a mismatch, EX\nmust redirect and restore the stack metadata so RVFI pc_wdata, ordering,\ntraps, and arbitrary symbolic instruction streams remain precise. Keeping\nthe lookup in ID confines the added muxing to the existing decode path while\nremoving return redirect bubbles on repeated CoreMark call sites.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r4s2\nAdd a one-entry last-target branch buffer indexed by an exact full-PC tag.\nOn a hit, fetch advances directly to the recorded target while the\ncurrent instruction carries prediction-valid and predicted-next-PC bits\nthrough ID/EX. Train the entry only from a valid EX-resolved taken branch,\nJAL, or JALR; invalidate it when a matching conditional branch resolves\nnot-taken. EX compares the carried prediction with the resolved next PC,\nsuppressing the normal redirect on a hit and redirecting plus flushing on\nevery miss. This preserves precise RVFI pc_wdata and recovery while\navoiding fetch-time opcode/immediate decode and limiting predictor muxing\nto one tag compare and target mux.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r5s2\nAdd a two-entry fully tagged branch-target buffer with a two-bit confidence\ncounter. Lookups use only the current fetch PC and registered tag/target\nstate, and predictions are enabled only for entries trained taken repeatedly;\nEX trains taken branches and jumps, decays confidence on conditional exits,\nand retains exact resolved-next-PC comparison. Carry predicted-valid and\npredicted-next-PC fields atomically through IF/ID and ID/EX so a hit retires\nwith the correct RVFI pc_wdata, while every miss or exit flushes and repairs\nthe fetch PC precisely. This targets recurring CoreMark loop redirects while\nlimiting aliasing, wrong-path churn, and IF critical-path logic.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r6s2\nAdd a one-entry exact-PC last-target predictor specialized for recurring\ncontrol-transfer PCs. IF uses the registered tag/target hit to fetch the\npredicted target, but carries only a one-bit prediction token with the\ninstruction; EX re-looks up the target and compares it with the resolved\nnext PC. A token is trusted only when the EX lookup still hits and agrees,\nso table replacement, not-taken exits, misaligned targets, and aliases\nalways take the normal precise redirect path. Train only on valid taken\nbranches and jumps, keeping the target mux and pipeline sideband smaller\nthan the prior metadata-heavy BTBs while removing steady-state loop flushes.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r7s2\nAdd a zero-state static predictor that recognizes only aligned RV32I JAL\ninstructions in IF, forms the J-immediate target, and fetches that target on\nthe following cycle. Carry only a one-bit predicted-JAL token with the\ninstruction; EX suppresses its normal JAL redirect only when that token is\npresent and the resolved target is aligned and agrees, while unpredicted or\nmisaligned JALs retain the existing precise redirect/trap path. Restricting\nthe fetch decode to opcode 1101111 avoids branch-history tables, JALR\nspeculation, and a general BTB, aiming to remove call/jump flushes with a\nsmaller Fmax and control penalty than earlier predictor attempts.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r8s2\nAdd a tiny direct-mapped loop-target cache trained only when a backward\nconditional branch retires. Each entry stores an exact PC tag, its resolved\ntarget, and an armed bit; IF uses a registered tag/target hit to redirect\nonly recurring loop branches. Carry only a compact cache-slot token with\nthe instruction, and protect the matching entry until EX resolves it so\ntable replacement cannot make an old prediction appear correct. A taken\nhit suppresses the normal EX redirect, while a loop exit invalidates the\nentry and follows the existing precise redirect path. Training at\nretirement prevents wrong-path branches from changing predictor state and\nthe loop-only scope avoids adding JAL/JALR traffic to the hot predictor.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r9s2\nAdd a one-entry exact-PC loop-target predictor that is trained only by a\nretiring, taken backward conditional branch and is enabled only after the\ntarget has been observed. IF performs just a tag compare and target mux,\nwith no opcode or immediate decode; the instruction carries a compact\nprediction token plus predictor epoch, while EX revalidates the same entry\nand suppresses its redirect only for an epoch-matched taken target. A\npredicted loop exit redirects to the fall-through path and invalidates the\nentry at retirement. Epoch-qualified lookup and retirement-only training\nkeep replacement, stalls, flushes, RVFI pc_wdata, and wrong-path state from\ncreating the pc_fwd failure seen in the earlier loop-target cache.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r10s2\nAdd a tiny one-entry last-target predictor trained only by retiring taken\nbackward conditional branches. On an IF hit, capture the predicted target\natomically with the instruction and use it for the next PC; carry that\nexact target with the instruction rather than re-reading mutable predictor\nstate in EX. EX suppresses the redirect only when the captured target,\nresolved taken target, and alignment all agree. Any loop exit, alias, table\nreplacement, or stale prediction therefore follows the normal redirect\npath, while eliminating the EX re-lookup and epoch-sensitive acceptance\nlogic from the prior attempts.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r11s2\nAdd a two-entry committed return-address stack that predicts only canonical\nJALR returns using x1/x5, with calls pushing pc+4 and returns popping only at\nretirement. Keep predictor state non-speculative: no checkpoints, epochs, or\nupdates from flushed instructions. Carry only a compact return prediction\ntoken and validate the captured target against the resolved JALR target and\nthe owning instruction PC; any miss, alias, non-return JALR, stall, or\nmisaligned target takes the existing precise redirect/recovery path. This\nshould remove recurring return flushes while limiting IF lookup and control\nfanout to indirect-return sites instead of burdening every branch.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r12s2\nAdd a zero-state decode-stage conditional-branch predictor/resolver. For a\nlegal conditional branch whose source operands are available through the\nexisting register-file/WB path, compute its exact direction and target in\nID and redirect the PC at the ID/EX capture edge; carry the resolved\nnext-PC and aligned/misaligned status with that branch so RVFI pc_wdata is\nexact. If either operand is an unresolved producer, or if the instruction\nis JAL/JALR or otherwise uncertain, suppress the early prediction and use\nthe existing EX resolution and forwarding path. This should reduce taken\nloop bubbles while avoiding speculative state, broad IF prediction\nmetadata, and unsafe recovery on stalls or misaligned transfers.\n\n\nhyp-20260710-003-r13s2\nAdd a certainty-only front-end predictor for unconditional JAL. When IF has\na valid JAL and its immediate target is aligned, form that target locally\nand advance the fetch PC directly, so the target instruction enters the\npipeline without waiting for the JAL's EX redirect. Do not add a predictor\ntable, history state, prediction-valid token, or EX agreement mux: JAL is\nalways taken, while misaligned targets fall back to the existing EX trap\npath. Keep conditional branches, JALR, bus stalls, forwarding, RVFI\npc_wdata, and retirement ordering on their existing paths. The narrow scope\nshould remove call/jump bubbles without burdening ordinary ALU fetches with\nbranch-history or target-table fanout.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
