ded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare predicted and resolved branch next PCs, redirect on misprediction, and retain misalignment/trap recovery semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the prediction-aware redirect and flush behavior through the existing pipeline control path.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 348.58, \"delta_pct\": 23.25, \"vs_baseline\": 23.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 150.22, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4309463, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9985, \"ff\": 1900, \"seeds\": [150.4, 150.22, 141.42], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:03:36.714593Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, +23.25%): A fetch-time BTFNT predictor can lose badly when `imem_data` branch decode, immediate formation, target addition, and prediction metadata extend the PC/IF critical path and recovery overhead enough to outweigh saved backward-loop redirects in CoreMark.\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic in-order data-memory completion queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM-stage handshake freezes PC, ID/EX, and EX/MEM whenever\\nio_dmemReady is low, so a transient data-bus stall blocks unrelated ALU\\ninstructions and directly lowers CoreMark instructions per cycle. The\\nrecent fetch FIFO result also shows that buffering only the front end is\\ninsufficient when the dominant stall is downstream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM-stage handshake freezes PC, ID/EX, and EX/MEM whenever\\nio_dmemReady is low, so a transient data-bus stall blocks unrelated ALU\\ninstructions and directly lowers CoreMark instructions per cycle. The\\nrecent fetch FIFO result also shows that buffering only the front end is\\ninsufficient when the dominant stall is downstream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the memory boundary as a small elastic in-order queue with a\\ndedicated completion/retirement head. Allow younger independent ALU\\ninstructions to execute into queue entries while an older load or store\\nwaits for io_dmemReady, but prevent younger memory operations from passing\\nan older memory operation and hold dependent consumers until the load data\\nis available. Retire only the queue head, preserving RVFI order, precise\\ntraps, register writeback, and architectural memory ordering. This should\\noverlap random dmem backpressure with useful integer work without adding a\\nfetch-time critical path or changing the external bus contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the metadata needed for elastic memory/completion entries, including age, completion, and deferred load/store state while preserving the existing RVFI payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single blocking MEM/WB boundary with a small in-order memory/completion queue that holds stalled data requests, accepts younger non-memory results, and exposes only the oldest completed entry to writeback and RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate queue-full, older-memory-order, and load-result dependency stalls instead of freezing the complete pipeline on every dmemReady low cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire elastic queue ready/valid flow through EX, MEM, WB, and retirement so dmem backpressure overlaps independent execution while redirects and RVFI remain precise.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the metadata needed for elastic memory/completion entries, including age, completion, and deferred load/store state while preserving the existing RVFI payloa