nfidence on conditional exits, and retains exact resolved-next-PC comparison. Carry predicted-valid and predicted-next-PC fields atomically through IF/ID and ID/EX so a hit retires with the correct RVFI pc_wdata, while every miss or exit flushes and repairs the fetch PC precisely. This targets recurring CoreMark loop redirects while limiting aliasing, wrong-path churn, and IF critical-path logic. \n\n hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 Tokenized exact-PC loop target predictor regression -1.34 401.44 173.55 None\nAdd a one-entry exact-PC last-target predictor specialized for recurring control-transfer PCs. IF uses the registered tag/target hit to fetch the predicted target, but carries only a one-bit prediction token with the instruction; EX re-looks up the target and compares it with the resolved next PC. A token is trusted only when the EX lookup still hits and agrees, so table replacement, not-taken exits, misaligned targets, and aliases always take the normal precise redirect path. Train only on valid taken branches and jumps, keeping the target mux and pipeline sideband smaller than the prior metadata-heavy BTBs while removing steady-state loop flushes. \n\n hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 Aligned JAL early-target predictor regression -6.48 380.51 169.03 None\nAdd a zero-state static predictor that recognizes only aligned RV32I JAL instructions in IF, forms the J-immediate target, and fetches that target on the following cycle. Carry only a one-bit predicted-JAL token with the instruction; EX suppresses its normal JAL redirect only when that token is present and the resolved target is aligned and agrees, while unpredicted or misaligned JALs retain the existing precise redirect/trap path. Restricting the fetch decode to opcode 1101111 avoids branch-history tables, JALR speculation, and a general BTB, aiming to remove call/jump flushes with a smaller Fmax and control penalty than earlier predictor attempts. \n\n hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 Retirement-trained loop target cache broken None None